    "prerequisites": {
      "status": "pass",
      "details": "Full 10.11 ending and the necessary 10.1–10.10 context provide feature dimensions, encoder learning, projector, insertion, single-shift labels and retrieval limits before 11 reuses them. 11 explicitly points back to answer masking, direct-SFT versus text-continuation examples and held-out attribute combinations; the actual 7.1/7.3/7.4/7.5/7.9–7.12/7.15–7.18 and T.4/T.6 bodies were read. 12 introduces samples, sample rate, frames, hop, window, complex STFT/power, overlapping bands and log scaling in order before the encoder; W.1–W.6 and needed tensor/probability/gradient/sampling sections supply the earlier mechanics. 13 introduces comparison data, answer-score sums, reference and gradients before DPO, then reward learning, advantage, ratio, clipping, value estimation and the assembled PPO step in order. Its introduction expressly permits a text-only route. Actual 14 introduction/full 14.1 makes Q/K and relative rotations explicit. Detailed prerequisite scope is preserved in reading-coverage.json; unlisted sections and recursively nested links are not claimed as read."
    },
    "terminology": {
      "status": "pass",
      "details": "Projector/feature width/position count retain distinct meanings; image and audio placeholders expand before one next-item shift, while render_chat already returns shifted X/Y. -100 consistently ignores direct answer loss and does not hide context. Gradients, trainable parameters, weight changes, teacher-forced loss and generated exact answers are treated as different evidence. Sample rate differs from amplitude normalization; STFT power differs from potentially negative log-mel values; centered framing accounts for encoder frame counts. Class labels, EOS, Unicode-character CER and byte-level text IDs are not conflated. DPO chosen/rejected are task-conditioned labels; its relative-to-reference margin differs from absolute chosen probability and generated correctness. PPO reward model, learned critic, per-batch old log probabilities and stage-fixed reference remain distinct, and clipping limits the old sample’s extra incentive rather than bounding every probability. All 30 rendered diagrams agree with those meanings; figure-view-receipts.json records personal explanations."
    },
    "examples_and_exercises": {
      "status": "pass",
      "details": "All code and exercises in 11–13 were read. Local width8 snippets explain plumbing, parameter masks, feature expansion or numerical gradients without presenting them as the width64 formal experiment. 11 partial-mask examples and T.6 general CLI masks are explicitly differentiated. Shape substitution with relabeling is separated from wrong-image/old-label testing; the 3/6→3/6 constant-circle case and 0/6 paired success explain why aggregate accuracy alone misleads. OCR introduces ordered multi-character targets and family grouping before exact/CER tests; the toy glyph demonstration differs from the RGB synthetic experiment. 12 exercises build from introduced waveform and spectral operations; changing encoder band count does not silently change the earlier independent display calculation. Silence agreement, pitch relabeling, joint-image/audio gradients and paired swaps have stated limits. 13 numerical DPO signs, beta examples, length bias, factual disagreement, PPO ratio/clipping/critic and finite-card assembly all have nearby definitions and explained outputs. Equal starting models/questions in PPO/DPO do not claim equal compute or generated-text superiority. No teaching kernel was rerun solely for evidence."
    },
    "source_runtime_scope": {
      "status": "pass",
      "details": "Direct SFT from fresh weights, continuation of the small attribute-text checkpoint, and later generic/pretrained natural-media models are explicitly separate. Chapter 11 visual/text scores retain their denominators and parent checkpoint scope; replay has greater token exposure and is not a matched-compute claim. Synthetic OCR groups strings and placements by family; test 2/30 exact, 40/57 CER and 30/30 EOS measure different things. Chapter 12 synthetic-tone results do not become real speech accuracy: FSDD uses 60 recordings, three speaker-specific 20-example partitions and a custom holdout, with test 3/20 and EOS20/20, not an official benchmark split. Resampling 8→16 kHz preserves duration and does not recover missing high frequencies; context extension is distinguished from learning speech labels. Chapter 20 uses a separate pretrained image-text base with optional LoRA and a separate selected ASR; actual validation can retain the unmodified base, and scene/OCR/order/chat/ASR scores are separated. The 13 PPO task selects complete predefined cards, with 0 LM effective tokens but nonzero sampled/reused actions; it does not continue the final generated-text model. Existing .venv was personally verified Python3.13.5/Torch2.14.1+cpu/CUDA false. Browser CPU outputs were observed existing executed-preview widgets, not claimed as reviewer reruns or GPU experiment reproduction."
